\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Real spinor-norm images and the low-rank boundary}

\p{Let #{Q} be a positive-dimensional finite-dimensional nondegenerate real
quadratic form. Distinguish the orthogonal norm #{\nu_Q^O} on #{O(Q)} from
its restriction #{\nu_Q} to #{SO(Q)}. Since
#{\mathbb R^{\times}/(\mathbb R^\times)^2=\{[1],[-1]\}}, their images and the
Spin image are:

##{\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{case}&\operatorname{im}\nu_Q^O&
\operatorname{im}\nu_Q&\text{Spin image}\\ \hline
\text{positive definite}&\{[1]\}&\{[1]\}&SO(Q)\\
\text{negative definite}&\{[1],[-1]\}&\{[1]\}&SO(Q)\\
\text{indefinite}&\{[1],[-1]\}&\{[1],[-1]\}&\ker\nu_Q
\end{array}}

For a positive form, every reflected value is a square, so the orthogonal
norm is trivial
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.orthogonalSpinorNorm_eq_one_of_posDef}. For
a negative form, the orthogonal norm is the determinant square class and is
therefore trivial on #{SO(Q)}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.orthogonalSpinorNorm_eq_orthogonalDetSquareClass_of_negDef}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinorNorm_eq_one_of_negDef}. In the
indefinite case, one positive and one negative reflection give a
determinant-one element of norm #{[-1]}, so #{\nu_Q} is onto
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinorNorm_surjective_of_indefinite}. Thus
the final kernel has index two. The Spin-image column follows from
\ref{fcap-0023}; the table does not identify this kernel with a topological
component.}

\p{Rank zero is separate: #{SO(Q)} is trivial, and the image/kernel theorem
uses its subsingleton branch rather than the positive-dimensional scalar row.
In rank one, #{SO(Q)} and its spinor norm are trivial. For any nonzero #{v},
the image of #{\nu_Q^O} is generated by #{[Q(v)]}, and it is trivial exactly
when #{Q(v)} is a square
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinorNorm_eq_one_of_finrank_le_one}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.orthogonalSpinorNorm_range_eq_zpowers_of_finrank_eq_one}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.orthogonalSpinorNorm_eq_one_iff_isSquare_apply_of_finrank_eq_one}.
An indefinite real form has rank at least two. Degenerate or semidefinite PGA
forms lie outside every row of this nondegenerate table.}
